In 1868, the American scientist Edward Drinker Cope rebuilt an *Elasmosaurus* skeleton and drew it with the head at the end of its tail. He had never seen a reptile with such a short tail and such a long neck, so he assumed it was the other way round. His old teacher, Joseph Leidy, pointed out the error, and his rival, Othniel Charles Marsh, made sure the story got around. Cope tried to buy up all the copies of the article.

The quarrel between them turned into the [[@bone-wars|Bone Wars]], a race to dig up and name more fossils than the other, which lasted for decades.

{{story:sea-turtles}}

Where the [[@monsoon|monsoon]] dumps huge amounts of rain, tropical evergreen forests grow. You'll find them along the [[@western-ghats|Western Ghats]], in the Northeast and on the Andaman Islands. Some trees here grow 45 metres or taller, about as high as a 15-storey building, and the leaves stay green all year.

Light hardly reaches the forest floor. Climbing plants, orchids and ferns cling to trunks to get closer to the sun. These forests hold many plants and animals found nowhere else, such as the lion-tailed macaque of the Western Ghats.

{{story:chipko}}

In Utah, the Great Salt Lake is a leftover from a much bigger ancient lake called [[@earth-salt-flats|Lake Bonneville]], which covered a huge area during the last [[@ice-ages|Ice Age]]. As the climate warmed and dried, it shrank, leaving the salt behind.

Today the lake's saltiness changes with the weather, and different parts have different amounts of salt. Brine shrimp, tiny animals about a centimetre long, thrive in it, and millions of birds come to eat them. A railway built across the lake in the 1950s split it in two, and the northern half became much saltier and turned [[@earth-weird-pink-lakes|pink with microbes]].

{{story:why-sea-salty}}

Galaxies come in a few main shapes. [[@maths-golden-spiral|Spirals]], like ours and Andromeda, are flat discs with curving arms full of [[@stars-colours|young blue stars]]. Ellipticals are smooth, rounded balls of older, redder stars, some of them giants. Irregular galaxies have no neat shape at all, often because they've been pulled about by a neighbour.

Galaxies also [[@galaxy-collisions|bump and merge]]. Many of the biggest ellipticals are thought to be built from several smaller galaxies that crashed together. Most big galaxies, including ours, have a giant [[@black-holes|black hole]] at the centre.

{{story:big-numbers}}

Maple seeds have a single wing and spin as they fall. In 2009, scientists showed that a small whirlwind forms along the front edge of the spinning wing, giving it extra lift and slowing its fall. On a breezy day, a seed dropped from a tall tree can land tens of metres away.

Many Indian trees use the same trick. Sal trees, which cover huge areas of central and northern India, drop seeds with several wing-like flaps that whirl to the ground. Mahogany seeds spin too. The drumstick tree, whose long pods go into sambar, makes seeds with three papery wings.

{{story:plants-move}}

Sometimes contrails disappear within seconds. Other times they stretch across the sky and spread out for hours. The difference is the air around them.

If the upper air is dry, the ice crystals turn back into vapour quickly, and the trail fades. If the air is already moist, close to forming its own clouds, the crystals survive and even grow. The trail can spread into wide sheets that look like natural wispy [[@clouds-types|clouds]] called cirrus. Old weather sayings that lasting trails mean rain may be coming have some truth to them, because they show moist air high up.

{{story:clouds-rain}}

In 1997, the hydrophones caught a sound they couldn't match to anything known. It came from the remote South Pacific, somewhere off the southern tip of South America.

The Bloop was so powerful that microphones thousands of kilometres apart heard it. Its pitch rose quickly, a pattern some animals use. That made people wonder if a living creature, far bigger than any known whale, was swimming in the deep. Scientists nicknamed it the Bloop and posted the recording online.

{{story:ocean-sounds}}

How does a light press of your foot stop a two-tonne car? The answer is fluid. Liquids can't be squashed, so a push on the fluid at one end comes out at the other. If the pistons at the wheels are wider than the one at the pedal, the force gets multiplied. Most cars also have a booster that adds extra help.

On Formula One cars, the brakes are so powerful that the carbon discs can heat up to around 1,000 °C. At night races you can see them glowing orange.

{{story:formula-one}}

In the 1850s, the English scientist Michael Faraday noticed that two ice cubes pressed together stick, even well below freezing. He suggested that ice has a thin, watery layer on its surface. For many years, other scientists doubted it.

Modern instruments that can study surfaces molecule by molecule have shown he was largely right. In 2018, scientists in Germany and the Netherlands reported that the top molecules of ice are loosely bound and can roll across the surface, almost like tiny ball bearings. They also found ice was slipperiest at about minus 7 °C. Colder than that, the surface grows stickier. In Antarctica, where ice can be colder than minus 40 °C, explorers found that sledges dragged across the snow almost as if it were sand.

{{story:ladakh}}

Much of the earliest Western music that survives was sung in churches. Monks sang plainchant, slow melodies in Latin, all on one tune with no harmony.

Around the year 1000, an Italian monk named Guido of Arezzo found a clever way to help singers learn new chants. He used a hymn in which each line began one step higher, and took the first syllable of each line: *ut, re, mi, fa, sol, la*. Later, *ut* became *do* and *ti* was added. He also helped develop the staff, the set of lines on which notes are written. Indian students learn the same idea with sa, re, ga, ma, pa, dha, ni.

{{story:maths-music}}

The oldest form of American local democracy comes from New England, in the north-east. Early English settlers in the 1600s, like the [[@pilgrims-mayflower|Pilgrims]], held meetings where the men of the community decided local matters together. Women and many poorer residents were left out at first.

The tradition survives. In Vermont, the first Tuesday in March is Town Meeting Day. In many towns, residents meet in a school gym or town hall, argue about the [[@budget-government|budget]], a new fire truck or a road, and vote. It is about as close as modern America gets to the [[@democracy-athens|direct democracy]] of ancient Athens.

{{story:pilgrims-mayflower}}

[[@new-zealand|New Zealand]] passed the first national minimum wage law in 1894. Australia followed soon after with wage boards. In 1907, an Australian judge ruled that a fair wage had to be enough for a worker to support a family, a famous decision known as the Harvester judgment.

In the USA, the [[@great-depression|Great Depression]] of the 1930s left millions working long hours for very little. In 1938, President Franklin D. Roosevelt signed the Fair Labor Standards Act, which set a national minimum of 25 cents an hour. The same law limited working hours and banned most [[@child-labour|child labour]]. Many workers then were not covered, including farm workers and domestic servants, and it took decades for the law to reach them.

{{story:great-depression}}

From around the middle of pregnancy, the ears begin working. By the last few months, babies can hear their mother's heartbeat and voice, along with muffled sounds from outside, like music and other people talking.

In 1980, the American psychologists Anthony DeCasper and William Fifer tested newborns only a few days old. The babies sucked on a special dummy, and by sucking in a certain rhythm they could choose which recording to hear. They worked to hear their own mother's voice rather than a stranger's. They had learned it before they were born.

{{story:echoes}}

The internet is very good at copying things. When you post a photo, it may be stored on several computers in different countries. Friends can share it, download it or take a screenshot in seconds.

Since 1996 the Internet Archive has been saving copies of public web pages in its Wayback Machine. In October 2025 it passed one trillion saved pages. Most of these are ordinary pages, but they show how much of the [[@internet-how|internet]] is remembered somewhere, even when the original is gone.

{{story:internet-how}}

ENIAC, built at the University of Pennsylvania and finished in 1945, was one of the first electronic computers that could be programmed for many tasks. It weighed about 30 tonnes and filled a large room. Its roughly 18,000 vacuum tubes used so much electricity that it gave off heat like a furnace.

Six women, including Kay McNulty, Betty Jennings and Frances Bilas, programmed it. For years their work was barely mentioned. Today they are recognised as pioneers of programming.

{{story:space-race}}

The part of the engine that draws, called the renderer, turns 3D shapes into flat images made of [[@pixels|pixels]]. Objects are built from thousands or millions of tiny triangles. The renderer works out where each triangle appears, what colour it should be, and how light and shadow fall across it.

This is so much work that computers have a special chip just for it, the graphics processing unit, or GPU. It can do many small sums at the same time.

{{story:gravity-fall}}

Engineers often draw a mobile network as a map of hexagons, like a [[@honeycomb|honeycomb]], with a tower in each. Real cells are messier, shaped by hills, buildings and roads, but the idea is the same. Each cell uses radio frequencies that are different from its neighbours', so calls don't interfere.

This lets frequencies be reused again and again across a country. In crowded cities, cells are small, sometimes just a few hundred metres across, so each tower serves fewer people. In the countryside, one tower can cover many kilometres.

{{story:honeycomb}}

The most common answer is a machine called a tokamak, first designed in the Soviet Union in the 1950s. It is shaped like a doughnut. Powerful [[@magnets-how|magnets]] wrapped around it create invisible fields that hold the plasma in a ring, floating away from the walls.

The biggest tokamak ever is being built in southern France. Called ITER, Latin for "the way", it is a joint project of more than 30 countries, including India, the United States, the European Union, China, Japan, [[@russia|Russia]] and [[@seoul|South Korea]]. India's share includes the cryostat, a giant steel vessel that surrounds the machine, built by the company Larsen & Toubro. India also runs its own smaller tokamaks at the Institute for Plasma Research in Gandhinagar, Gujarat.

{{story:sun-what}}

Wilbur spent hours watching birds such as buzzards glide. He saw that they kept their balance by twisting the tips of their wings. One day, while idly twisting a long empty cardboard box in the shop, he realised that wings could be twisted the same way. They called this wing warping.

From 1900 they tested gliders at Kitty Hawk, chosen for its steady winds and soft sand dunes for landing. The gliders didn't lift as well as published tables said they should. So in 1901 the brothers built a small wind tunnel in their shop and tested about 200 model wing shapes. Their own measurements were far better than anyone else's at the time.

{{story:how-birds-fly}}

The Gupta smiths had no giant furnace. They made iron in small lumps, each perhaps a few kilograms, in clay furnaces burning charcoal. Then they hammered the hot lumps together, one after another, into a single pillar. This is called forge welding.

Their way of making iron left tiny bits of slag (glassy waste) and lots of phosphorus inside. In modern [[@steel|steel]], makers try hard to remove phosphorus because it can make steel brittle. For the pillar, it turned out to be a gift. Similar iron beams were used in temples such as [[@konark|Konark]], so these smiths knew their craft.

{{story:rust}}

The temple has a door on each of its four sides. The [[@sikh-gurus|Sikh Gurus]] wanted to show that God is everywhere and welcomes everyone, from every direction.

A long marble causeway leads across the water to the temple itself. Visitors cover their heads, take off their shoes and wash their feet before entering. Inside, singers called ragis perform hymns from the Guru Granth Sahib, accompanied by harmonium and tabla.

{{story:sikh-gurus}}

Eight states sit in the northeast, joined to the rest of India by a narrow strip of land. This is one of the greenest, rainiest parts of the country. [[@assam-tea|Assam grows tea]] along the Brahmaputra river, [[@meghalaya|Meghalaya]] has some of the wettest places on Earth, and Arunachal Pradesh reaches up into high mountains.

Dozens of communities live here, speaking many languages. Food often uses [[@northeast-food|bamboo shoots]], smoked meats and fermented ingredients you won't find on a menu in Delhi. [[@sikkim|Sikkim]], tucked between Nepal and Bhutan, became known for farming entirely without chemical fertilisers.

{{story:languages-india}}

To get form V, chocolate makers temper it. They melt it fully, cool it while stirring so crystals start to form, and then warm it slightly, to around 31°C for dark chocolate. The gentle reheating melts away the weak crystals and leaves form V, which then acts like a template, teaching the rest of the fat to line up the same way.

Get it wrong by even a degree or two and the bar turns out streaky. Professional chocolatiers check by smearing a little on a cold knife or a strip of paper and watching how it sets.

{{story:body-temperature}}

In south India, meals are often served on a banana leaf. In [[@kerala|Kerala]], the Onam sadya is a famous feast with over 20 dishes, from sambar and avial to payasam. Each dish has its own place on the leaf, and the leaf is laid with its tip pointing to the guest's left.

{{story:onam-pongal}}

Banana leaves are clean, waterproof and leave nothing behind when thrown away. Some people also say food tastes better on them.
